<p>This course is about the arts. But it is not a course in art history, and it is not a studio course where you learn to paint or sculpt. It is a course in the humanities, which means we will be asking a specific kind of question about the arts: what do they reveal about human values?</p>
<p>That question sounds abstract, but it isn't. A painting of a screaming child in a junkyard says something about war. A photograph of a tired woman holding her children says something about poverty and resilience. A building designed to make you feel small says something about power. The arts take ideas that are difficult to put into words and give them a form you can see, hear, or feel. This course will teach you how to pay attention to that process.</p>
<p>This unit lays the groundwork. It introduces the concept of values, distinguishes the humanities from the sciences, and begins to develop a vocabulary for talking about artistic form and structure. These are tools you will use in every unit that follows.</p>

<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>Learning Objectives</p>
<p>After completing this unit, you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Explain what the humanities are and how they differ from the sciences</li>
<li>Describe the role of the humanities in clarifying values</li>
<li>Distinguish between an emotional response to a work of art and a fuller, more informed perception of it</li>
<li>Explain what artistic form and structure are, and why they matter</li>
<li>Understand how abstract ideas can be expressed through concrete images</li>
</ul>




